   COULD THIS TEST HAVE SEEN IT?
   measured 1 sigma on alpha (the phase-misaligned quadrupole per unit predicted monopole) = 0.6182
   95% exclusion on a misaligned quadrupole, from end-to-end injection into
   the real data: |alpha| < 1.158, i.e. a misaligned quadrupole ratio below 0.93,
   i.e. e_kappa < 2.11.  The geometric maximum of e_kappa is 1, so NO
   external-axis tensor of any amplitude is excluded by this sample.
   measured SOURCE-ALIGNED quadrupole per unit predicted monopole: +0.0749 +- 0.1076
      that is the SUM of the baryonic quadrupole and any source-axis or network
      tensor.  The two cannot be separated -- which is the degeneracy the phase
      channel exists to avoid, and the reason an amplitude alone decides nothing.
      aqual|tensor_T|tidal|inv|m2|I1e-33                  
         eps_K = 1.000, predicted a2c/a0 = 0.2565, predicted a2s/a0 = 0.0000
         source axis: the quadrupole is locked to the baryonic axis, so it lands in a2c where it is degenerate with baryonic ellipticity; a2s power is ZERO by construction
      aqual|tensor_d|tidal|inv|m2|I1e-33                  
         eps_K = 1.000, predicted a2c/a0 = 0.0000, predicted a2s/a0 = 0.0000
         K grad Phi_N = exp(2AW/3) grad Phi_N EXACTLY -- the field direction is an eigenvector, so this tensor cannot turn the flux and predicts no quadrupole of any phase.  It is a scalar rescaling wearing a tensor's name.
      aqual|tensor_d|tidal|inv|m4|I1e-33                  
